analysis:
Examined the BASE text of docs/operations/provider-release.md (lines 1-904) and the changed ranges. The PR edits only this document. Head text is not supplied, and the patch excerpt is truncated.

Change 1 (base 174, head 174-176): SDK 27 release preparation is described as a daily schedule (`47 6 * * *`), gated on provider-swift/** and libs/** changes, or as a manual dispatch. The doc says it uses no signing or publication secrets. No security impact is visible.

Change 2 (base 497-532, head 499-541): the Dev-Branch Lane changes from push-triggered with no workflow_dispatch (base 497-500) to a 6-hour schedule (`23 */6 * * *`) plus manual workflow_dispatch on refs/heads/dev. Because schedules run only from master, a `gate` job on ubuntu-24.04 without secrets is described.

This changes the trigger model for Developer-ID-signed dev builds. Base 534-538 says dev pushes get the production Developer ID, and base 254-262 says dev builds meet the same self-updater and installer designated requirement as prod. The base source gate (base 245-252, 517) accepts only a push to refs/heads/dev that is GitHub-verified and on dev ancestry. A scheduled run (ref=master) or a dispatch would not match that push-only gate.

The workflow, the resolver and verify_workflow_source changes are not in this batch. I cannot verify how the schedule or dispatch path:
- binds the dev SHA to a verified commit on dev ancestry;
- keeps `secrets: inherit` limited to DEV_* publication secrets;
- keeps the prod lane unreachable.

These are relevant to the artifact-identity concerns in A-011, A-017, T-013 and T-054. Documentation alone is not evidence of a regression, so no finding is filed. Integration must check:
- .github/workflows/release-dev-branch.yml (gate job permissions, checkout ref, SHA pinning);
- scripts/provider_release_resume/api.py verify_workflow_source for schedule and dispatch events;
- scripts/resolve-provider-release.sh refusal of prod/staging on non-tag triggers;
- who can dispatch the workflow.

No threat-model text references dev-lane triggers, so no threat-model update is clearly required.
